Even in C, you need the "class" definitions.  The major
differences with C++ is that you have an occasional public: or
private: (usually one each per class), and the function
declarations are in the class in C++, but outside of it (and
take one more parameter) in C.  So before inheritance, we're
down to two extra lines per "class" in C++.  With inheritance...
implementing the equivalent functionality in C takes a lot more
lines.

I have experienced the fact that programs are often bigger in
C++ than in C, but this has always been because they did more.
You end up implementing things in C++ that you wouldn't have
tried in C, because they'd have been too complicated.

Anyhow, that's my experience.

